["10:00"]={
{"At about \003ten o’clock\004 the first tiny snowflakes came loitering down and settled on Jill’s arm. Ten minutes later they were falling quite thickly. In twenty minutes the ground was noticeably white.","The Silver Chair","C.S. Lewis",true},
{"Shanghai’s clocks were set an hour ahead so the city could “save daylight”, but the Bai family said: “We go by the old clock.” \003Ten o’clock\004 to them was eleven to everyone else. Their singing was behind the beat; they couldn’t keep up with the huqin [Chinese string instrument] producing bleak and desolate sounds of life.","Love in a Fallen City","Eileen Chang",true},
{"It was at \003ten o’clock\004 to-day that the first of all Time Machines began its career. I gave it a last tap, tried all the screws again, put one more drop of oil on the quartz rod, and sat myself in the saddle. I suppose a suicide who holds a pistol to his skull feels much the same wonder at what will come next as I felt then.","The Time Machine","H.G. Wells",false},
{"If he jumps, he thinks, he will plummet, a slow sweep through empty space, a horrible way to die. He feels, momentarily, the heat of the impact, the ghostly wet of his skull collapsing. The illusion of weightlessness gives way. The elevator rebounds shut with a clang.\nIt’s a little after \003ten a.m.\004 on a Saturday.","Real Life","Brandon Taylor",false},
{"The \003ten-o’clock\004 breakfasters began to appear: nervous, little men, morose, preoccupied, who wiped their plates with crusts of bread; rude, massive women who, like primitive idols dug out of the soil, had grown rotten in the years; flowery dandies with repulsive faces, reminding him uncomfortably of illustrations in medical tracts.","Crazy Cook","Henry Miller",true},
{"There are some good things about living alone. The room is cool, but cool air tones up the skin. One nice thing about her job is that it doesn’t start until \003ten\004, which gives her a long morning, time to grow slowly into her day.","The Robber Bride","Margaret Atwood",true},
{"At \003ten o’clock\004, he went to Shinjuku and bought a Fujitsu word processor with his credit card. It was the latest model, far lighter than earlier versions.","1Q84, Book One","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"It was \003ten o’clock\004. And now he is dead, and the police will be called in, and I shall be suspected of having had a hand in it. Oh, yes, I am sure I shall.","The Sign of Four","Sir Arthur Conan Doyle",true},
{"Billy is ready to go early the next morning, but Bucky asks him to wait until \003ten o’clock\004 because he has an errand to run. While he does it, Billy visits the summerhouse one final time. He takes the picture of the hedge animals off the wall and carries it to the end of the path. He looks out over the gorge for a minute or two, across to the place where the reputedly haunted hotel once stood.","Billy Summers","Stephen King",true},
{"That first day was a madhouse, all right. I ran the Carolina Spin with Lane until \00310 o’clock\004, then alone for the next ninety minutes while he rushed around the park putting out opening day fires.","Joyland","Stephen King",true},
{"At around \003ten\004 every morning, my wife and I would take a cooler down to the beach. We’d lather up with sunblock, then sprawl out on mats on the sand. I’d listen to the Stones or Marvin Gaye on a Walkman, while my wife plowed through a paperback of Gone With the Wind. She claimed that she’d learned a lot about life from that book. I’d never read it, so I had no idea what she meant.","Blind Willow, Sleeping Woman","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"“My mother in law’s not a bad person. I don’t have any negative feelings toward her. It’s just that she’s the nervous type, and has always relied too much on other people. Do you understand the situation?”\n“I think so,” I said.\nShe crossed her legs, waiting for me to write something new on my pad. But I didn’t write anything down.\n“She called us at \003ten\004 one Sunday morning. Two Sundays—ten days—ago.”\nAt ten I turned off the TV, went back to my room, and went to sleep.","Blind Willow, Sleeping Woman","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"She spent that night in Junpei’s room. They drank wine—a gift from the restaurant—had sex, and went to sleep. When Junpei woke at \003ten o’clock\004 the next morning, she was gone, leaving only an indentation like a missing memory in the pillow next to his, and a note: “I have to go to work. Get in touch with me if you like.” She included her cell phone number.","Blind Willow, Sleeping Woman","Haruki Murakami",false},
{"A new life opening up for me at the Villa Borghese. Only \003ten o’clock\004 and we have already had breakfast and been out for a walk.","Tropic of Cancer","Henry Miller",true},
{"Anyway, when he looked at his watch again it was \003ten o’clock\004. At ten o’clock she was lying on the divan with her boobies in her hands. That’s the way he gives it to me—in driblets.","Tropic of Cancer","Henry Miller",false},
{"The heat had started in the small hours of the morning, swiftly building up. Around \003ten o’clock\004, it had fully erupted into being, just after Turks and Greeks on each side of the Green Line had finished their morning coffees.","The Island of Missing Trees","Elif Shafak",true},
{"By \003ten o’clock\004 she was ravenous. She cooked herself a huge brunch and ate every bite. Then she took her movie back to Blockbuster and asked if they had \001Kiss of Death\002. They didn’t, but after ten minutes of browsing, she settled on a substitute called \001Last House on the Left\002.","Full Dark, No Stars","Stephen King",true},
{"\003Ten o’clock\004 the mail comes up. Sometimes you get the torn envelope.","One Flew Over the Cuckoo’s Nest","Ken Kesey",true},
{"Frodo woke and found himself lying in bed.\n“Where am I, and what is the time?” he said aloud to the ceiling.\n“In the House of Elrond, and it is \003ten o’clock in the morning\004,” said Gandalf. “It is the morning of October the twenty-fourth, if you want to know.”","The Fellowship of the Ring","J.R.R. Tolkien",true},
{"They brought him back to the ward about \003ten in the morning\004—“Fulla piss an’ vinegar, buddies; they checked my plugs and cleaned my points, and I got a glow on like a Model T spark coil. Ever use one of those coils around Halloween time? \001Zam\002! Good clean fun.”","One Flew Over the Cuckoo’s Nest","Ken Kesey",false},
{"Hours of sitting and watching, and all she had to report back to the hungry beaks of Ballinacroagh’s \003ten o’clock\004 parishioners was a sinful Benny Corcoran and a bunch of dirty tinkers. It was simply not good enough for a week’s work.","Pomegranate Soup","Marsha Mehran",false},
{"It was \003ten in the morning\004, and Lars was just about to make the drive to the old butcher shop to acquire the key ingredient for the surprise when Amy Jo and Wojtek Dragelski’s Mazda 626 pulled into a guest parking space outside.","Kitchens of the Great Midwest","J. Ryan Stradal",true},
{"They were to call for her at her house in the Taurida Gardens at \003ten o’clock\004, but it was already five minutes to ten, and the girls were not yet dressed.\nNatásha was going to her first grand ball. She had got up at eight that morning and had been in a fever of excitement and activity all day.","War and Peace","Leo Tolstoy",true},
{"Meanwhile, the blood of the city’s saints had taken on a whole range of properties hitherto unknown to science. One lady’s was liquefying at precisely \003ten o’clock\004 every Tuesday, while San Giovanni’s bubbled away obligingly whenever it heard Holy Scripture.","The Wedding Officer","Anthony Capella",true},
{"In assaying to put on his regimental coat and waistcoat, my uncle Toby found the same objection in his wig, so that went off too; So that with one thing and what with another, as always falls out when a man is in the most haste, ’twas \003ten o’clock\004, which was half an hour later than his usual time, before my uncle Toby sallied out.","Tristram Shandy","Laurence Sterne",true},
{"’Tis but \003an hour ago since it was nine\004, And after one hour more ‘twill be eleven.","As You Like It","William Shakespeare",true},
{"For some seconds the light went on becoming brighter and brighter, and she saw everything more and more clearly and the clock ticked louder and louder until there was a terrific explosion right in her ear. Orlando leapt as if she had been violently struck on the head. \003Ten\004 times she was struck. In fact it was ten o’clock in the morning. It was the eleventh of October. It was 1928. It was the present moment.","Orlando","Virginia Woolf",true},
{"The trial was irretrievably over; everything that could be said had been said, but he had never doubted that he would lose. The written verdict was handed down at \00310:00\004 on Friday morning, and all that remained was a summing up from the reporters waiting in the corridor outside the district court.","The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo","Stieg Larsson",true},
{"According to military records no US bombers or any other kind of aircraft were flying over that region at the time, that is around \00310 am\004 on November 7, 1944.","Kafka on the Shore","Haruki Murakami",true},
{"At about \003ten o’clock\004 in the morning the sun threw a bright dust-laden bar through one of the side windows, and in and out of the beam flies shot like rushing stars.","Of Mice And Men","John Steinbeck",true},
{"I went to bed and the next thing I knew I was awake again and it was getting on for \003ten o’clock\004 in the morning. Ring, ring, said the telephone, ring, ring.","The Medusa Frequency","Russell Hoban",true},
{"If Wednesday should ever come! It did come, and exactly when it might be reasonably looked for. It came — it was fine — and Catherine trod on air. By \003ten o’clock\004, the chaise and four conveyed the two from the abbey; and, after an agreeable drive of almost twenty miles, they entered Woodston, a large and populous village, in a situation not unpleasant.","Northanger Abbey","Jane Austen",true},
{"King Richard: Well, but what’s o’clock?\nBuckingham: Upon the stroke of \003ten\004.","Richard III","William Shakespeare",true},
{"Monday 30 March 1668 Up betimes, and so to the office, there to do business till about \00310 o’clock\004","The Diary of Samuel Pepys","Samuel Pepys",true},
{"On July 25th, 8:30 a.m. the bitch Novaya dies whelping. At \00310 o’clock\004 she is lowered into her cool grave, at 7:30 that same evening we see our first floes and greet them wishing they were the last.","The Terrors of Ice and Darkness","Christoph Ransmayr",false},
{"The pundit sighed. “Only a fool like me would leave his door open when a riot can occur at any moment, and only a fool like me would say yes to you,” he said. “What time?”\nJust his head was sticking out of the partially opened door. The money from blessing the ice-cream factory must have dulled his desire for work, I thought.\n“\003Ten\004.”\n“Ten-thirty.” Without another word, he closed the door.","An Obedient Father","Akhil Sharma",true},
{"The Saturday immediately preceding the examinations was a very busy day for Kennedy. At \003ten o’clock\004 he was entering Willey’s room; the latter had given him a key and left the room vacant by previous arrangement — in fact he had taken Olivia on another house hunting trip.","The Greeks Have a Word for It","Barry Unsworth",true},
{"The summer holidays were near at hand when I made up my mind to break out of the weariness of school-life for one day at least. With Leo Dillon and a boy named Mahoney I planned a day’s mitching. Each of us saved up sixpence. We were to meet at \003ten in the morning\004 on the Canal Bridge.","Dubliners","James Joyce",true},
{"Well, I shall arrive in Concordia on the 27th, at \003ten in the morning\004; I am traveling by train. I have the numbers, the addresses, etc., but I do not think I shall need them.","Artificial respiration","Ricardo Piglia",true},
{"It is one of those cinemas that stay open twenty-four hours a day: it was \003ten in the morning\004 and I went in to forget the cold for a while.","Artificial respiration","Ricardo Piglia",true},
{"He was seen at \003ten in the morning\004 getting off the train that arrives from the capital. He paused on the station steps, a little disoriented, and asked which way the river was.","Artificial respiration","Ricardo Piglia",true},
